                                Abstract
The fast incorporation of Distributed Energy Resources (DERs) and P2P trading is 
revolutionizing today's power systems by transitioning them into decentralized cyber-physical 
systems. However, they also present an inherent conflict between visibility and cybersecurity 
of vital operational data, which creates potential weaknesses in the network and exposes the 
grid to complex threats such as false data injection and false command injection. For detecting 
this type of cyber-attacks, the project suggests a novel approach towards developing a cyber
physical power system (CPPS) architecture in which P2P trading occurs while implementing 
effective security strategies.  
The proposed system is based on the IEEE 13-bus distribution feeder and involves using 
a central controller to link the physical network with the blockchain layer, encrypting all 
measurement data through blockchain-supported encryption. In order to protect the network 
from the cyberattacks, the proposed scheme utilizes an integrated approach for threat detection 
that blends both traditional statistics and machine learning models. By coupling Weighted Least 
Squares (WLS) and covariance analysis with LSTM networks and PINNs, the intelligent 
system is able to differentiate between noise and malicious behaviour within the network.  
This project has proven to be useful in detecting attacks such as FDI and FCI aimed at 
corrupting the grid state as well as subtle attempts at manipulating system operations. The 
framework also provides a solution to data availability risks through the deployment of a Q
learning algorithm for SDN-enabled rerouting that guarantees continuous network connectivity 
for total grid observability. Upon confirmation of any attack, the blockchain component 
initiates key rotation and device revocation actions to isolate and mitigate potential damage to 
both the physical power flow and the digital market.
